  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We wish to express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{690}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle $\\theta ={690}^{∘}.$\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{690}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the corresponding coterminal angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$ We do this by subtracting integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get our desired angle.\n\n$$\n{690}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}={330}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{690}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253971/assets/fa525528e916f927a3bf6a8742e1a5abda17fb8c24d19adbfadf25d220804d5f.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta ={330}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}={360}^{∘}-{330}^{∘}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The tangent function is negative $(-)$ in the fourth quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{690}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{690}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}.\n$$",
